<p>Field staff roles typically include a company car, gas card, laptop, phone and benefits worth roughly $8,000 to $15,000 a year. Plug your own figures into the <a href="/salary-calculator/">Adjuster Salary Calculator</a> to compare offers.</p>

<h2 id="how-hurricane-seasons-drive-florida-pay">How hurricane seasons drive Florida adjuster pay</h2>
<p>Atlantic hurricane season runs June 1 through November 30, and Florida's adjuster economy follows it. A single landfall can produce hundreds of thousands of claims, and carriers cannot staff for that peak year-round. They rely on independent adjusting firms to deploy CAT adjusters within days.</p>
<p>For independent adjusters, that means:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deployments of two weeks to several months</strong>, often working 7 days a week and 12-hour days.</li>
<li><strong>Per-claim or daily pay.</strong> Fee schedules tied to gross loss, or daily rates commonly around $400 to $700 for desk and daily CAT work, and $500 to $800 or more for experienced field and commercial adjusters.</li>
<li><strong>Fee splits</strong> with the IA firm, commonly 65/35 to 75/25 in the adjuster's favor.</li>
<li><strong>Deployment earnings</strong> commonly ranging from $15,000 to $60,000 or more.</li>
</ul>
<p>A quiet season is the flip side. Florida IAs who rely only on hurricanes can see slow years in the $30,000 to $60,000 range, while big storm years can push experienced CAT adjusters past $150,000. Those top years are rare, not typical. Estimate a deployment with the <a href="/cat-deployment-calculator/">CAT Deployment Earnings Calculator</a>, and see our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/cat-adjuster-salary/">CAT adjuster salary</a> guide for a deeper breakdown.</p>

<h3>Staff vs independent in Florida</h3>
<p>Staff adjusters at Florida carriers often get pulled onto storm duty too, but they keep their regular salary, benefits and vehicle, sometimes with overtime or CAT pay added. Independents trade that security for upside: when claim counts spike, they can work as many files as they can handle. Many Florida adjusters spend a few years on staff learning property claims, then go independent once they have the skills and savings to ride out a quiet season.</p>

<h2 id="florida-salary-by-city">Insurance adjuster salary: Florida by city</h2>
<p>These are estimates for mid-level staff adjusters. Specialty and employer matter more than location, but each metro has its own character.</p>
<h3>Miami</h3>
<p>South Florida has the state's highest living costs and the most litigation, which pushes casualty, bodily injury and large loss pay upward. Mid-level staff adjusters commonly see $65,000 to $88,000.</p>
<h3>Tampa</h3>
<p>Tampa Bay has a large concentration of carrier and claims operations plus serious Gulf storm exposure. Typical staff pay runs around $60,000 to $82,000.</p>
<h3>Orlando</h3>
<p>Central Florida hosts major claim centers for auto and property and is a common landing spot for trainees. Expect roughly $58,000 to $78,000 for experienced staff roles.</p>
<h3>Jacksonville</h3>
<p>Jacksonville is home to several large insurance and claims employers and tends to have lower living costs. Staff pay commonly falls between $57,000 and $78,000.</p>
<p>See how Florida compares nationally on our <a href="/salary-by-state/">insurance adjuster salary by state</a> page.</p>

<h2 id="florida-vs-texas-adjuster-pay">Florida vs Texas Adjuster Pay</h2>
<p>Many working adjusters hold both licenses. Texas is the most common designated home state license because of its broad reciprocity, and a Florida license adds access to the country's heaviest hurricane market. Staff pay in both states sits close to the national BLS median of $76,790; the bigger difference is the type and timing of storm work.</p>

