<h2 id="florida-6-20-license-and-emergency-licenses">The Florida 6-20 license and emergency licenses</h2>
<p>Florida residents who adjust claims for insurers or independent firms generally need the <strong>6-20 all-lines adjuster license</strong>. It covers property, casualty and most other lines, which is why it is so widely held. You complete pre-licensing education or pass the state exam, submit fingerprints and apply, then complete continuing education to renew.</p>
<p>Florida also issues <strong>emergency adjuster licenses</strong> after declared disasters. These temporary licenses let carriers and firms bring in out-of-state help quickly, but they are tied to a sponsoring employer and expire. If you want to work Florida storms year after year, a permanent license (resident 6-20 or a non-resident license) puts you at the front of the deployment list.</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Non-residents often pair a Texas designated home state license with a Florida non-resident license to cover both major hurricane markets. A Xactimate certification and roof inspection experience make you far more attractive to Florida IA firms.</div>

<h2 id="roofing-claims-in-florida">Roofing claims: the backbone of Florida property work</h2>
<p>Outside of named storms, Florida property adjusters spend a large share of their time on roof claims: wind damage, aging shingles, tile roofs, and the water intrusion that follows. Florida's building code, roof age rules at many carriers and frequent disputes over repair versus replacement make these files detail-heavy.</p>
<p>Adjusters who can confidently inspect steep and tile roofs, document damage cleanly and write accurate Xactimate estimates are the ones who earn top dollar in Florida. That skill set carries straight into hurricane deployments, where roof and water claims make up a big share of the workload. Read more in our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/property-adjuster-salary/">property adjuster salary</a> guide.</p>

<h2 id="public-adjuster-fee-caps-in-florida">Public adjuster fee caps in Florida</h2>
<p>Public adjusters represent policyholders and are paid a contingency fee from the settlement. Florida generally caps that fee at 20%, and at 10% for claims tied to a declared state of emergency during the year after the declaration. On a $100,000 hurricane claim filed in that window, the PA fee is limited to $10,000.</p>
<p>Florida has also passed several rounds of property insurance reform in recent years aimed at reducing litigation and stabilizing the market. If you plan to work as a PA, expect strict rules on solicitation, contracts and fees, and stay current through continuing education.</p>
<p>Public adjusters nationwide commonly earn $50,000 to $100,000 a year, with established PAs handling commercial and large losses earning $150,000 or more. Florida offers plenty of claim volume, but also intense competition and close regulatory scrutiny.</p>

